          "changeMeaning": "Spawn the basic vector A and grid layout to introduce students to vector representation.",
          "narration": "Welcome to the physics lab on Vectors and Projectile Motion. A vector represents a physical quantity with both magnitude\u2014its length\u2014and direction. Here, Vector A is represented as an arrow pointing diagonally on our tabletop coordinate grid."
        },
        {
          "clipName": "clip1_decomposition",
          "audioClip": "decomposition_explained",
          "autoAdvance": false,
          "changes": [
            {
              "target": "vector_A_x",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "vector_A_y",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "text_label_Ax",
              "enabled": true,
              "components": [
                {
                  "componentType": "textMeshPro",
                  "text": "Component A_x = A * cos(theta) = 4.0",
                  "fontSize": 30
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "target": "text_label_Ay",
              "enabled": true,
              "components": [
                {
                  "componentType": "textMeshPro",
                  "text": "Component A_y = A * sin(theta) = 3.0",
                  "fontSize": 30
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "Spawn orthogonal components to show vector decomposition visually.",
          "narration": "To analyze motion in multiple dimensions, we decompose a vector into perpendicular horizontal and vertical components. By projecting Vector A onto the X and Y axes, we find its components: A_x of 4 units and A_y of 3 units, forming a perfect right triangle."
        },
